%N A137310 Numbers k such that a type-4 Gaussian normal basis over GF(2^k) exists.
%C A137310 A type-t Gaussian normal basis exists for GF(2^k) if and only if p = k*t+1 is prime and gcd(k, (p-1)/ord(2 mod p)) = 1.
%C A137310 Note that p = k*t+1 being prime and gcd(k, (p-1)/ord(2 mod p)) = 1 imply that (p-1)/ord(2 mod p) divides t. As 2 is not a quadratic residue a prime congruent to 5 modulo 8, k is a term if and only if 4*k + 1 is a prime congruent to 5 modulo 8 with primitive root 2 (A213050). - _Jianing Song_, Oct 05 2026
%H A137310 Jianing Song, <a href="/A137310/b137310.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A137310 Joerg Arndt, <a href="https://www.jjj.de/fxt/#fxtbook">Matters Computational (The Fxtbook)</a>, section 42.9 "Gaussian normal bases", pp.914-920.
%F A137310 A213050(n) = 2*A163777(n) + 1 = 4*a(n) + 1. - _Jianing Song_, Oct 05 2026
%o A137310 (PARI) isA137310(k) = my(p); (isprime(p=4*k+1) && znorder(Mod(2, p)) == p-1) \\ _Jianing Song_, Oct 06 2026
%Y A137310 Cf. A071642 (type-1), A054639 (type-2), A136415 (type-3), A137311 (type-5), A137313 (type-6), A137314 (type-7), A101284 (type-8).
%Y A137310 Cf. A163777, A213050.
